Concept
	What I had in mind for this assignment was to reference my game off of the video game called “Crazy Taxi,” which had been released around the late 1990s to 2000s on a variety of platforms. However, for my game specifically, the goal would be a fast food delivery service (i.e. similar to Uber Eats or DoorDash). The objective of the game is to make as many fast food deliveries within the allotted time frame. 

	For Power-Ups, the player can collect coins, which would add 10 points to their total score. Collecting blue cubes would add 30 seconds to the timer. Green cubes would double the player’s current score. I also had the idea of the car being able to jump, and adding a turbo boost in mind, but I was not able to implement it within the time frame of this assignment.

	For Upgrades, the player can change up to five different colors for their car during gameplay, as well as adding two different sticker decals to their car. I had in mind of allowing the player to change the body shape of the car, however I was not able to come up with a solution for this particular requirement.

	For Obstacles, the player must avoid colliding into buildings, pedestrians, garbage cans / dumpsters, other vehicles and walls. If they do, then 100 points will be deducted from their score.

	For Level of difficulties, the player can choose the level mode in the Option menu at the Start Menu. The level of difficult is set on Hard Level by default, if they do not click on the Option menu at the beginning of the game. In Easy Mode gameplay would only last for 1 minute. In Medium Mode gameplay would only last for 3 minutes. In Hard Mode, gameplay would last for 5 minutes. 

	The start menu, provides an Option button, as well as a Tutorial button, which explains the purpose of the game, game rules, game controls, as well as game visuals, to indicate to the player of what to look out for.


Sprites and Audio Sources
	Other than the “ARCADE - Free Racing Car” asset used in the project, I had managed to create all of my sprites and audio sources using GIMP, and Apple’s GarageBand softwares. All of my prefabbed objects were done in the Unity Editor.


